NXR-251|DMA late-completion isolation|DMA and release|DMA qualification shall reject a completion for an old ticket even after cancellation and a later transfer starts.|A device completion bit without transfer identity can corrupt the next owner's result.|Replay old completion before and after new completion; only the current generation changes state or data.|Use an independently scripted backend and physical delayed IRQ where available.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One active ticket per channel; generations never wrap within object lifetime.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|A device completion bit without transfer identity can corrupt the next owner's result.
NXR-252|DMA quiescence proof boundary|DMA and release|A DMA backend shall report completed or stopped only when no further bus writes from that operation can occur.|The portable channel relies on backend quiescence guarantees that host state tests cannot prove.|Delay the final write beyond the completion indication; qualification fails a backend that declares quiescence early.|Instrument bus writes or FPGA markers and compare final access against ownership release.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite drain deadline; uncertain stop enters quarantine.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|The portable channel relies on backend quiescence guarantees that host state tests cannot prove.
NXR-253|DMA cache-line exclusivity|DMA and release|DMA buffers shall own all cache lines touched by prepare and finish maintenance operations.|Correct byte bounds do not prevent cache maintenance from touching neighboring objects.|Adjacent application canaries survive transmit and receive tests with unaligned requested lengths.|Exercise first and last cache-line boundaries on a cached board with independently observed buffer contents.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Staging alignment and rounded capacity cover the hardware cache-line size.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|Correct byte bounds do not prevent cache maintenance from touching neighboring objects.
NXR-254|DMA failed-start side effects|DMA and release|A backend start failure shall guarantee that no transfer was issued and no application buffer remains hardware-owned.|The interface's false-start guarantee is essential to safe immediate reuse.|A false start result followed by any bus activity fails; retry preserves channel ownership and counters.|Script rejected starts and monitor bus activity or FPGA transfer counters.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|No application pointer retained; fixed channel staging allocation.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|The interface's false-start guarantee is essential to safe immediate reuse.
NXR-255|DMA quarantine recovery domain|DMA and release|A quarantined channel shall remain unavailable until the declared reset domain proves hardware access has ended.|Software state reset cannot stop a bus master that still owns memory.|Repeated begin and cancel calls cannot release quarantined staging; reset of an unrelated peripheral does not recover it.|Inject stop failure, attempt reuse and verify only the qualified reset clears ownership.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Staging remains reserved during quarantine; no automatic generation reset.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|Software state reset cannot stop a bus master that still owns memory.
NXR-256|No silent hardware skips|DMA and release|Qualification shall distinguish passed, failed, unsupported, not-run and incomplete for every required board-profile test.|A green aggregate is misleading if unsupported hardware tests disappear from the denominator.|Remove required UART checker or unavailable PMP support; aggregate release status cannot remain passed.|Validate result aggregation with mixed result fixtures and missing required evidence.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite declared test matrix; no implicit default-pass entries.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|A green aggregate is misleading if unsupported hardware tests disappear from the denominator.
NXR-257|Evidence-qualified feature claims|DMA and release|Release notes shall link every supported feature and board claim to its configuration and retained acceptance evidence.|Source presence and generated requirements are not sufficient evidence of target support.|Delete or mismatch an evidence fingerprint and the corresponding qualified claim is rejected.|Cross-check release manifest, requirement IDs, board identities and evidence hashes.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One finite support matrix; planned work is never promoted by documentation generation.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|Source presence and generated requirements are not sufficient evidence of target support.
NXR-258|Fair RTOS comparison profile|DMA and release|Any published NexRT comparison shall match workload semantics, optimization, allocation, clock and enabled services across compared systems.|Small default images cannot fairly be compared with another RTOS configured for additional services.|An unmatched interrupt profile or omitted feature cost invalidates the comparison label.|Retain complete build inputs, measurement method and raw repeated samples for every participant.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Comparison scope and memory accounting fixed before measurements.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|Small default images cannot fairly be compared with another RTOS configured for additional services.
NXR-259|Task requirement closure audit|DMA and release|Each qualification task shall retain a stable requirement link, specific acceptance criteria, dependency list and unresolved evidence status.|The earlier HIL design CSV was outside the canonical StrictDoc export and needs explicit implementation task traceability.|Duplicate IDs, missing acceptance, cyclic dependencies and stale generated task text fail the repository check.|Run negative tracker fixtures and tamper with one generated task to prove stale-output detection.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Exactly one task per imported qualification review; at most 100 in this campaign.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|The earlier HIL design CSV was outside the canonical StrictDoc export and needs explicit implementation task traceability.
NXR-260|Ten-review milestone integrity|DMA and release|The campaign shall build and verify each ten-review boundary before making its local milestone commit.|Counting authored rows is not equivalent to executing 100 runtime engineering changes and must remain labeled as requirements review.|Boundaries 10 through 100 each retain command logs, catalogue hash and successful exit codes; a failed command prevents commit.|Run the documented local checkpoint workflow and verify milestone ancestry without network operations.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-159|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Ten bounded milestones; no push; source and build evidence retained separately.|include/rtos_dma.hpp,src/dma.cpp,tests/dma.cpp,docs/dma.rst|Counting authored rows is not equivalent to executing 100 runtime engineering changes and must remain labeled as requirements review.
